      "content": "# Register the XD Pool provider. Discovery is read-only: the plugin reuses the\n# WorkBuddy desktop app's own sign-in files and never starts a separate OAuth\n# flow. Requests rotate across every discovered account.\n- insert:\n    - id: llm-xdpool\n      name: dsh-xdpool\n# billion-context is deliberately NOT mounted by this layer.\n#\n# 2.0.0 tried to. It inserted a row (`billion-context-xdpool`) so a profile that\n# had not installed the compressor would get one for free, and `src/billion-mount.ts`\n# held a runtime `create()` as a second path for the same job. Measured against a\n# profile that had ALREADY installed billion-context — which is the normal state\n# for anyone using that feature, since `bili-native` comes from billion-context's\n# own `dsh.bundle.patch.yml` — neither safeguard held:\n#\n#   the host did not disable our duplicate row, and it did not resolve it to a\n#   no-op. It left `enabled: true` and ran the fiber to `failed`, because\n#   billion-context registers a cordis service under its own name and a second\n#   registration is refused outright (`service \"…\" has been registered at <…>`).\n#\n# So every user who had installed billion-context got a permanently failed entry\n# in their plugin list — visible, unexplained, and pointing at us.\n#\n# The row below is kept, but DISABLED, for three reasons:\n#\n#   1. Addressability. `disabled: true` keeps the row's id resolvable, so a\n#      profile that wants to flip it on can do so with one edit instead of\n#      inventing a row.\n#   2. It states the decision where the old attempt lived, where the next person\n#      to wonder \"why doesn't the pool bring up the compressor?\" will look.\n#   3. It keeps `bili-native` (billion-context's own row) as the ONLY mount of\n#      that module, which is the arrangement that actually works.\n#\n# CONSEQUENCE, STATED PLAINLY\n#\n# A profile that has not installed billion-context sees the billion card report\n# \"not installed\". That is the honest reading of the facts, and it is already a\n# first-class state on that page — the page distinguishes not-installed from\n# no-answer from ready precisely so this case reads as information rather than\n# as a bug. Installing billion-context remains a decision the user makes; the\n# pool no longer makes it for them.\n#\n# `src/billion-mount.ts` no longer calls `create()` for the same reason: with\n# this row disabled, a runtime create would fight the very row it sits beside.\n# The function still runs — it is what lets the card distinguish\n# already-installed from not-installed — it just never mounts anything.\n- id: billion-context-xdpool\n  disabled: true\n"
